{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261113T182019Z-a4ff9ded", "ts": "2026-11-13T18:25:45Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/native-review-inner", "build": "1151816623d4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "1151816623d479208aff6fa0d759c2fbc7edc0e0", "base_tree": "5ab18a4021f5600a6184059948214b556aefbb98", "head_tree": "c4550c07189f860f5fb30556bba2b0a2ce8e9766", "head_commit": "a2ab09340f1bfd036394cb31d683561fe002d915", "dispatch_commit": "a2ab09340f1bfd036394cb31d683561fe002d915", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_native_review_compare.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/changes/native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_native_review_compare.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/changes/native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The two new native_review manifest refusals (mode, synthetic_range) have no test", "recommendation": "Instance. `_validate_native_review` now rejects a block whose `mode` is not `cumulative`/`chunk`/`verify-resolutions` and one whose `synthetic_range` is not a bool. The accept side runs end to end, because `begin_review` validates its own sampled-inner manifests. Neither refusal is ever fed a case it must catch. `TestManifestValidation` pins every other refusal (each FORBIDDEN token, each missing key) and skips these two. So if the mode check were deleted or inverted, a manifest block claiming a `final` pairing would validate silently and the suite would stay green. Add two parametrized cases in the existing `TestManifestValidation` pattern: `mode=\"final\"` and `synthetic_range=\"yes\"` must each return `(False, ...)` naming `native_review`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "tree_moved borrows the coverage gate's cost predicate to answer a different question, and its documented meaning is broader than the code", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: `native_review._tree_moved` filters the moved files through `coverage_algebra.judgeable_files`, and that predicate answers 'does an edit here re-open the coverage gate?', not 'did a file the native review read change before the run was recorded?'. `coverage_algebra.is_review_subject`'s docstring names this exact move as a defect: a cost predicate given a second job. Two consequences follow. (1) Coupling: `tree_moved`'s meaning now changes whenever the coverage gate's free-edge rules change (the `.md`-only-when-governance-protected rule, and any future COV-3M8Q relaxation), with nothing in `native_review` to notice. (2) Under-reporting: every plain `.md` the native review reads is excluded, e.g. `plugin/docs/*.md`, README, and this interval's own `governance-telemetry.md` and `project-state-keys.md`. A builder who fixes a reviewed doc while the native run is still going gets `tree_moved: false`, and `compare.py` prints no WARNING for that run. The plan's mid-build decision justifies the exclusion with 'written during any review \u2014 consolidation itself writes them'. That is true of `.prawduct/` records and not of plain docs. Meanwhile `plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md` documents the field as true when 'a file in the reviewed interval differed', which the code does not do. Fix: give the question its own predicate in `native_review`, excluding only what consolidation and the builder's bookkeeping write during a review (`coverage_algebra.RECORD_PREFIXES` / `gitstate.METADATA_PREFIXES`), and make the governance-telemetry sentence name that exclusion. Pin it with a test where an edit to a reviewed plain `.md` sets `tree_moved: true`. If excluding plain docs is really intended, state that exclusion in governance-telemetry.md rather than 'a file'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "review-stats' native_review block now blends inner and boundary runs into one count and one median, and nothing in the bundle looked at it", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the change widens which runs produce `native.review` (one in six inner reviews joins every cumulative) but never re-checks the aggregates over that event. The learnings rule 'Changing how data ARRIVES (scan\u2192incremental, snapshot\u2192log) silently re-scopes every aggregate' fits here exactly. The event's readers are `telemetry._native_block`/`_native_line` and compare.py, found by grepping `NATIVE_EVENT` / `native.review` across plugin/lib. compare.py was updated to split by stage. telemetry.py was not, and it is not in the plan's `depends_on`. `review-stats` still prints 'native review (paired, #1130; in no total here): N run(s) | duration median ...' over one pooled population. Before this change that population was all boundary `cumulative` runs. Once the bundle lands it mixes in smaller inner runs, and the Critic numbers beside it are split `by_stage`. Who goes wrong: whoever reads `review-stats` for the native run's cost or duration. The plan's own figure ('a native `high` run (67-99s measured)') and its revisit trigger ('if a month's spend surprises; the sample rate is one constant') are read this way, and that reader gets a blended median and run count with no way to see what the inner sample added. Fix: key `_native_block` by the event's `native.stage`. Events from before this change have no stage, so count them as boundary, as `record` and compare.py's `stage_of` already do. Render one line per stage and pin it with a test. Alternatively, record in governance-telemetry.md that the block is pooled across stages.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md:10", "recommendation": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 4 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md:16", "recommendation": "nonfunctional-requirements carries 4 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 3 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md:21", "recommendation": "data-model carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 3 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md:26", "recommendation": "api-contract carries 3 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 2 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-7"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "next_action_clause now drops the boundary clause when the sidecar is unreadable, and no test covers it", "recommendation": "Before this change the clause fired whenever a sidecar file existed. Now it goes through `_read_sidecar`, so a sidecar that is corrupt, or whose id does not match, returns \"\" even for a boundary run. This is advice failing soft, and `record` refuses the same sidecar anyway, so the effect is small. Still, it is a behaviour change on the boundary path that the plan does not list. A one-line test would pin which way it should go.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "A verify-resolutions pass over an empty interval can still pair a native run", "recommendation": "`begin_review` lets `verify-resolutions` dispatch with an empty `files_changed`. When such a pass falls in the sample, `native_review_range` names an empty range: real commits that are equal, or a synthetic head with the base's tree. That costs a native fork that reviews nothing, and it puts a trivially clean inner pair into compare.py's inner tables. Gating the pairing on a non-empty `files_changed` would avoid both.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Docstring of test_a_dev_final_writes_no_block_and_says_nothing describes a mechanism that no longer holds", "recommendation": "The docstring says a `final` would skip the block even without the mode guard, because it has no head commit. `native_review_range` now names a synthetic range in exactly that case. The test still catches a missing mode guard through its `native_review not in manifest` assertion, so only the explanation is wrong. Delete the mechanism sentence; no other change is needed.", "files": ["tests/test_native_review_pairing.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The per-stage wall-clock lines from Chunk 02 are not asserted", "recommendation": "Chunk 02 says wall clock is reported per stage, and compare.py prints `wall clock, <stage> \u2014 Critic: ...; native: ...`. No test in test_native_review_compare.py checks that line. The stop-condition and inner-pair lines are asserted. A per-stage assertion would close the gap, using the `inner_pair` fixture, whose partials carry durations.", "files": ["tests/test_native_review_compare.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The sample-rate comment justifies one-in-six by a stop condition that does not count inner pairs", "recommendation": "The comment above `NATIVE_REVIEW_INNER_SAMPLE` in `plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py` says one in six 'still reaches #1130's sixty within its four weeks at the measured rate'. The same change makes `compare.py`'s stop condition count boundary pairs only, so inner pairs never advance the sixty. The rationale therefore points at a threshold the sample cannot move, and it also carries an undated measured-rate claim. Nothing reads the comment, so no edit is owed. If it is touched, delete the 'reaches the sixty' clause, or make it relational (what volume of inner pairs the comparison needs).", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "write_dispatch's mode default is unreachable", "recommendation": "`write_dispatch` reads `block.get(\"mode\", \"cumulative\")`, but its only caller, `begin_review`, passes the block `native_review_block` built moments earlier in the same call, and that block always carries `mode`. The legacy-sidecar fallbacks in `record` and `next_action_clause` are the defaults that actually serve the upgrade case. This one is dead and can be indexed directly. No other action is wanted.", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Framework-specific checks: review-cycle.md manifest line", "recommendation": "The one skill-file edit, the `native_review` clause in `review-cycle.md` 'Dispatch manifest', matches the code: every cumulative, plus sampled chunk and verify-resolutions, on development builds. It is clear and contradicts no other critic skill surface. A grep of `plugin/skills/critic/` finds no other sentence restricting pairing to cumulative, and the dispatch instruction is the code-printed `native_review_line`, which is already mode-neutral. Generality, Instruction Clarity, Cumulative Health and Pipeline Coverage raise nothing. No action.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "compare.py's moved-tree warning treats `tree_moved: null` as not moved", "recommendation": "`_tree_moved` returns None for 'could not be told' and says so ('unknown, never \"no\"'). compare.py's `moved` list only names runs where the value is truthy, so a run whose check failed is counted as comparable without any note. The README's step 1 promises that every warning names runs where the tree assumption failed. Events from before this change carry no field at all, so a separate count of runs with a null `tree_moved` among inner pairs would keep those runs visible. This is an oracle file and clears no Records Pass bar. Noted for the experiment's reader only.", "files": [".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/compare.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "next_action_clause now goes silent when a boundary sidecar is unreadable", "recommendation": "Before this change, a sidecar file that existed was enough for the clause to name the native list. Now `_read_sidecar` returning None also returns '', so a corrupt boundary sidecar drops the builder's only pointer to a native text that `native-review show` could still read. `record` fails on the same sidecar and reports why, so the failure is not invisible. It is just no longer reported where the builder reads. Reading a None sidecar as a boundary run would restore the old behaviour.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`.prawduct/.native-reviews/` keeps growing with no lifecycle, now faster", "recommendation": "Each paired run leaves a sidecar and a text per clone, and nothing removes them. compare.py needs all of them for the blind sheet, so keeping them is deliberate while the experiment runs. The inner sample adds roughly eighty pairs a month at the plan's measured rate. That is harmless until the experiment ends, but nothing then says what becomes of the directory. The unreachable commits are covered by `git gc`, as recorded.", "files": ["plugin/lib/native_review.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: the plan's Verification Strategy says the boundary table must agree with the previous run's, and the change meant it not to", "recommendation": "Chunk 02's verification reads 'a run over the paired reviews recorded so far, whose boundary table must agree with the previous run's'. Matching against observations deliberately moved findings from native-only to shared (the change fragment says six), so the shared and native-only columns were bound to differ. Only the counts of Critic findings are invariant. The plan is complete and nobody acts on this sentence, so it clears no bar and no edit is wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: the change fragment ships a count with no command that re-derives it", "recommendation": "'On the first five paired reviews, six findings moved from native-only to shared' goes into the release notes as a bare number. The learnings rule 'A spike that discards its code leaves its numbers unfalsifiable' asks for the command, not the digits. Here the script is committed and the sentence is dated by its fragment, so it is a dated measurement and not a stale present-tense claim. I could not re-derive it without executing compare.py. It clears no bar.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/native-review-inner.md"], "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": "native-review-inner", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "1151816623d479208aff6fa0d759c2fbc7edc0e0", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/changes/native-review-inner.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02", "03"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md", "findings": [{"check": "governed-by-gap", "path": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-native-review-inner.md", "line": 10, "detail": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 4 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one"}, {"check": "governed-by-gap